#!/bin/sh
# icq-session — keep GtkICQ's OSCAR session alive across restores and idle pauses.
# Lives in the guest at /root/bin/icq-session and is launched from ~/.xinitrc
# INSTEAD of a bare `gtkicq &`. This copy is the source of truth.
#
# WHY A TIMER AND NOT A WATCHDOG. slackware's watchdog
# (docs/lab/retronet/STATION-slackware.md) restarts its client when the client
# EXITS. GtkICQ 0.60 gives you no such edge in the case that matters:
#
#   * It has NO reconnect path at all — there is no "reconnect", "auto connect"
#     or re-login string anywhere in the binary, and no Status-menu item that
#     re-logs in.
#   * When the gateway reaps its legacy session (ICQ_LEGACY_SESSION_TIMEOUT,
#     120 s, by design) the client does NOT notice and does NOT exit. It keeps
#     painting "Ready (Online)" and its full contact list while every packet it
#     sends is answered `V4 packet from unknown session, sending NOT_CONNECTED`.
#     That is the exact failure a `loadvm golden` produces, and it is invisible
#     on the framebuffer — the exhibit looks perfect and is talking to nobody.
#   * It DOES exit promptly when it cannot resolve or reach the server at
#     STARTUP ("Couldn't determine hostname"), which is why the loop also
#     self-heals a guest that comes up before the gateway does.
#
# So the session is refreshed on a timer instead. 100 s keeps every session
# inside the 120 s reaper with margin. A fresh start re-logs in with no GUI step:
# gtkicqrc's Server is "icq.mirabilis.com", which the retronet DNS resolves to
# the gateway 10.99.0.2, and the Contacts section carries HiveBot — which is why
# the contact MUST be in the file and not only in the running process.
while :; do
  gtkicq &
  p=$!
  sleep 100
  kill $p 2>/dev/null
  wait $p 2>/dev/null
  sleep 1
done
